WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.740
If you like the Witcher video games, there's
a lot you'll like on the Netflix series, too.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.280
But if you fell in love with Geralt, Ciri,
Yennefer and the rest with a controller in

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.009
your hand, you'll find a few surprises waiting
in the TV adaptation of these classic Polish

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:13.720
books.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:19.440
One of the best characters in CD Projekt Red's
games and Andrzej Sapkowski's novels is the

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:24.220
fashionable, womanizing bard Dandelion, who's
somehow one of Geralt's closest friends despite

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.040
his penchant for getting into trouble.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.480
"He could die."

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.340
"*gasp* F---.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:28.940
Geralt —"

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:30.660
"Uh… yeah.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:31.720
We won't let that happen."

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.080
He's just as delightful in the TV show, although
you might not immediately recognize him.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.100
In Netflix's The Witcher, he goes by a different
name.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.600
Instead of Dandelion, The Witcher calls the
character Jaskier.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.660
"Well, that makes sense, insomuch that it
sort of… doesn't."

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.100
There's a good reason for this.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.859
In the novels' original Polish, "Jaskier"
is actually Dandelion's name, and translates

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.260
in English to "Buttercup."

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:00.879
In the games, the name Dandelion fit well,
but the bard's flamboyant personality has

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.140
been toned down for the show.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.020
Jaskier is still a scene-stealing rascal,
but he's not quite as extravagant as the Dandelion

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:07.780
in the games.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.600
The Netflix show generally has a more serious,
grounded tone, and a silly name like Dandelion

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.000
would stick out like a sore thumb.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:18.660
"Your man might have mentioned that the road
was too narrow for horses in his original

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:19.440
sales pitch."

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:21.540
"Welcome to the world, Jaskier."

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.810
For many fans, Doug Cockle is Geralt of Rivia.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:29.860
The actor has played the Witcher for a dozen
years, voicing Geralt all three games, the

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:35.020
card-based spinoff Gwent, and in guest appearances
in Soulcalibur 6 and Monster Hunter: World.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.920
"Come on, let's do this."

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.300
Before Henry Cavill took the lead role for
the Witcher TV series, he was just another

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.660
fan of the video games, and he didn't copy
Cockle's performance for his take on the character.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:51.270
For one, Cavill is British, and in the show
he speaks with something close to his natural

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:51.760
accent.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:56.260
His tone of voice is also deeper than Cockle's,
whom Cavill has described as having more of

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:57.260
a whispery delivery.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:02.280
"I wasn't going to make him American, like
Doug Cockle performed him in The Witcher.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.539
And so I kind of blended my natural accent
with that gravelly tone that Doug uses."

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.500
Cavill's Geralt doesn't sound the same, but
it still sounds right.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:10.900
That's what really matters.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.000
"It's not easy being a Witcher.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.320
Trust me — I'd know."

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.010
In the Witcher games, Geralt carries two swords;
There's the steel one for regular fights,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.100
and the silver one for fighting monsters.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:26.260
In the show, that hasn't changed, but you're
not going to see Geralt with two swords strapped

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:27.320
to his back very often.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.380
For the most part, Cavill's Geralt only carries
around one sword at a time.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.840
That's a note that comes straight from the
books, in which Geralt tends to leave his

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.600
silver sword on his horse, Roach, unless it's
a very special occasion.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.220
It's also a practical concern, according to
the Witcher show's supervising armorer, Nick

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:44.060
Jeffries.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:49.120
As Jeffries said in an interview with Polygon,
silver is a soft and flimsy material, and

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.560
wouldn't do much good during battle.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.880
It's also pricey, and would attract bandits.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:54.700
Geralt already has enough trouble.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:55.980
He doesn't need more.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.040
In fact, all of the weapons and costumes on
the show are more grounded than their video

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.240
game counterparts.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:05.500
What works in a digital world doesn't always
work in live action, and the costume designers

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:10.130
behind the TV show deliberately turned away
from games' style, making costumes and accessories

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:11.480
more grounded and practical.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.060
They may not be quite as visually memorable,
but they hold up a lot better in a fight.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.680
The Witcher has a lot of backstory to get
through, and unlike the games, it has to appeal

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.540
to an audience that's not always willing to
buy into fantasy.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.380
Series showrunner Lauren Schmidt Hissrich
told Looper,

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.600
"You don't want to alienate people who've
never heard of The Witcher before.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.500
[...] I wanted to make sure I was giving it
in bite sized-pieces."

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.570
As such, some of the characters that you know
from the games have been combined, or given

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:37.320
new roles.

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:42.080
For example, the wizard Mousesack or Ermion,
as the game calls him can be found in The

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.900
Witcher 3, which takes place after the events
of the TV series and the books.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:50.360
He won't play a similar role in the show,
though, since - spoiler alert - he's killed

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.700
halfway through the first season.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.510
You're going to run into that sort of thing
a lot familiar characters or personalities,

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:59.060
but remixed in a way that makes them feel
fresh or new.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.240
Triss Merigold is one of the very first people
you meet in the first Witcher game, where

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.400
she's positioned as Geralt's main love interest.

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:09.840
In The Witcher 3, when Geralt's on-again,
off-again girlfriend Yennefer finally shows

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.420
up, the game embraces a legitimate love triangle.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.950
Players have to choose which of the two sorceresses
they want to romance.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:17.570
Both make a strong case.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:20.440
Just don't try to double-dip in the end, you'll
regret it.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:25.180
Well, if you're team Triss, we have some bad
news: While Yennefer is a major character

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:29.580
on the show, Triss is only a bit part, and
she and Geralt don't have many sparks.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:33.980
As far as Netflix's series is concerned, Yennefer
is Geralt's one and only.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:38.700
It's a shame to lose all that juicy drama,
but honestly, The Witcher is busy enough as

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:39.200
it is.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:44.920
A love triangle is a nice way to break up
a 90-hour game, but show only has eight episodes.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.340
There are more important things to spend that
time on.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:52.680
If you only know Geralt and Yennefer's romance
from The Witcher 3, you probably think a genie

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:53.780
made them fall in love.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:58.340
After all, that's the main thrust of the sidequest
"The Last Wish," which serves as a sequel

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:00.940
to the Sapkowski short story of the same name.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.420
In the quest, Geralt and Yennefer hunt a djinn
in order to undo the wish that bound the two

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:05.920
together.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.620
When you succeed, you can either continue
romancing Yennefer or dump her.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:10.640
It's your call.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.140
The game implies that magic made Geralt and
Yennefer get together.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.100
On the show, it's a lot more complicated.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:17.600
Like, a lot.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:22.120
Episode five, "Bottled Appetites," depicts
Geralt and Yennefer's first meeting, and while

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:27.110
it does feature Geralt saving Yennefer's life
by calling on a djinn, the wish itself is

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:27.820
ambiguous.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.960
As in the books, you don't hear exactly what
Geralt wished for.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.940
Further, it's pretty clear in the show that
Geralt has feelings for Yennefer.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.960
The elf Chireadan flat-out says as much, and
Geralt and Yennefer's mutual attraction turns

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:41.560
out to be important to the plot.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:46.020
Geralt risks his life for Yennefer because
he likes her, and Yennefer's flirty dialogue

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.800
hints that she feels the same way.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:51.300
The genie might seal the deal, but the sparks
are already flying.

